# Caddy — the only thing on the Phase A box listening to the world.
#
# `docs/08` §3: "Ingress | Caddy on the box (as the desk does today)". §2 keeps the same shape in
# Phase B, where CloudFront → ALB replaces this file and nothing else moves.
#
# THE GATE
# --------
# Maulik chose a gated staging host (26 Aug 2026) because the four legal drafts under
# `src/content/legal/` are unreviewed (NEEDS-MAULIK §19) and D3's regulatory posture is written
# but UNREVIEWED. Until counsel clears them, an India-equities site must not be publicly
# discoverable. Three independent mechanisms, because any one of them can be misconfigured:
#
#   1. ~~`basic_auth`~~ — REMOVED 27 Aug 2026 (M46.4). Google's OAuth verification fetches the
#      homepage and the legal pages anonymously and refused the app while they answered 401.
#   2. `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` on every response — written as "the one that still works
#      if auth is ever lifted before the drafts are cleared". That day arrived; this is now load
#      bearing rather than a belt.
#   3. `/robots.txt` served from here, disallowing everything, overriding whatever the app emits.
#
# So the site is readable by anyone holding a URL and still not discoverable through a search
# engine. `noindex` governs indexing, not fetching, which is why a verification crawler can read
# what a search crawler is told to ignore.
#
# Flipping public is `docs/runbooks/07-deploy-phase-a.md` §7 and is deliberately more than one
# edit: removing the password should not be something a fat finger can do.
# Both of these are whole *directives*, not values, and both may legitimately be empty.
#
# Caddy has no conditionals: `email {$BASKFY_ACME_EMAIL}` with the variable unset expands to a
# bare `email` and the config fails to adapt ("wrong argument count"), which is how the first
# version of this file crash-looped. A placeholder that expands to nothing is a blank line, which
# the parser is happy with — so the switch is the presence of the directive, not of an argument.
#
#   BASKFY_ACME_EMAIL_DIRECTIVE="email ops@example.com"
#   BASKFY_ACME_CA_DIRECTIVE="acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory"
#
# Use the staging CA while DNS is still moving: a failed challenge against the production
# endpoint burns Let's Encrypt's rate limit for a week. Neither is needed when
# BASKFY_SITE_ADDRESS is a bare port (local verification), because Caddy does not attempt ACME
# without a hostname to get a certificate for.
{
	{$BASKFY_ACME_EMAIL_DIRECTIVE}
	{$BASKFY_ACME_CA_DIRECTIVE}
}

{$BASKFY_SITE_ADDRESS} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the gate
	#
	# Caddy evaluates directives in ITS OWN order, not the order they appear in this file. Writing
	# `basic_auth` above the `handle` blocks did not put it "first" — it put it first for every
	# path, including /robots.txt and /healthz, both of which came back 401. Matchers, not
	# position, are how you scope it.
	# `route` forces these to run in the order they are written. Without it Caddy applies its own
	# directive table, which puts `basic_auth` ahead of `header` — the 401 short-circuited before
	# the header handler ever ran, so the one response a stranger actually receives was the one
	# response missing `X-Robots-Tag`. Verified by `tools/deploy/verify-gate.sh`.
	route {
		# ---------------------------------------------------------- the gate is gone (M46.4)
		#
		# `basic_auth` and its `@open`/`@gated` matchers were here. Maulik removed the password on
		# 27 Aug 2026 so that staging is reachable, which Google's OAuth verification requires: it
		# fetches the homepage, the privacy policy and the terms anonymously and refused the app while
		# each of them answered 401. Six of its eight complaints were this gate.
		#
		# TWO OF THE THREE MECHANISMS REMAIN, AND THEY ARE NOW THE WHOLE DEFENCE
		# The header block above this file's own introduction describes three independent layers. The
		# first is now removed. The remaining two are `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow` on every
		# response, set below, and a `/robots.txt` that disallows everything. Together they mean the
		# site is *readable by anyone holding a URL* and still not *discoverable* through a search
		# engine — which is the property the original decision was actually protecting, because the
		# four legal drafts under `src/content/legal/` are unreviewed (NEEDS-MAULIK §19) and D3's
		# regulatory posture is written but UNREVIEWED.
		#
		# The introduction says flipping public "is deliberately more than one edit: removing the
		# password should not be something a fat finger can do." It was not one: it was asked for
		# explicitly, after the consequence was put in writing and read. What that sentence is really
		# asking of the next person is that these two layers not be removed casually as well —
		# `tools/deploy/verify-gate.sh` fails if either one goes.

		# `noindex` unconditionally, and set on the way IN rather than deferred.
		#
		# `defer` was tried first — it applies the header when the response is written, which is
		# what you want for overriding a value a backend sends. But `basic_auth` writes its 401
		# through its own error path, and the deferred writer never ran: the single response a
		# stranger actually receives was the only one without the header. Setting it eagerly puts
		# it on the shared ResponseWriter before any handler downstream can short-circuit.
		#
		# This is the mechanism that still holds if the password is ever lifted before counsel
		# clears the legal drafts, so it is the one that must not depend on anything else being
		# right. `tools/deploy/verify-gate.sh` asserts it on the 401 specifically.
		header {
			X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet"
			# Caddy announces itself; one less fingerprint on a box that reaches the order path.
			-Server
		}


		# ---------------------------------------------------------------- open paths
		# Served from here rather than by the app: this file has to be true even when the app is down,
		# and `apps/web` legitimately serves a permissive robots.txt for the day this goes public.
		handle /robots.txt {
			header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
			respond `User-agent: *
Disallow: /
` 200
		}

		# Uptime checks need something without a password, and it must reveal nothing.
		handle /healthz {
			respond "ok" 200
		}

		# ---------------------------------------------------------------- routes
		# The API, for the browser. Server rendering does not come through here at all — the web
		# container reaches `http://api:8000` directly across the compose network
		# (`BASKFY_INTERNAL_API_ORIGIN`), which is why a gated host does not 401 its own pages.
		#
		# `/api/v1/*`, NOT `/api/*`. FastAPI serves only under the version prefix, and Next owns
		# three sibling routes that live under `/api` without it:
		#
		#   /api/auth/[...nextauth]   NextAuth — session, sign-in, callback
		#   /api/revalidate           on-demand ISR
		#   /api/account/export       the GDPR export stream
		#
		# The first draft matched `/api/*` and handed all three to FastAPI, which answered 404.
		# The visible symptom was `GET /api/auth/session 404` in the browser console and a broken
		# session on every page — a bug that looks like NextAuth misconfiguration and is a one-word
		# routing error. Widening this back to `/api/*` breaks authentication site-wide.
		# ------------------------------------------- the reverse Kite bridge (leaf 1.1.5)
		#
		# Maulik moved the Kite Connect app's ONE registered redirect to
		# `https://staging.baskfy.com/api/v1/brokers/callback` (31 Aug 2026). The desk at
		# 65.0.226.77 still places every live order and must rebalance on Friday, and a Kite
		# `request_token` is single-use — so exactly one of the two systems can redeem it.
		#
		# It has to be the desk, and that is a fact about the deployed desk rather than a
		# preference: its `app/main.py` caches the Kite client in a module global and
		# `app/kite_client.py` reads the token file only in `Kite.__init__`, so a token written
		# to its disk after uvicorn started is never seen by the process that places orders
		# (`tools/deploy/desk/prove-live-token-pickup.py` demonstrates both halves). Driving the
		# desk's own `/callback` updates that live client in place, with no restart — and
		# `momentum-web.service` is `Restart=on-failure` under root, so a restart is not
		# something this side could safely cause anyway.
		#
		# Baskfy loses nothing: `baskfy_worker.kite_session_cli pull` borrows the access token
		# back from the desk over SSH minutes later, exactly as it does today (M58). One app,
		# one redirect, one login, two consumers — the same sentence M58 was built around, with
		# the login landing here first.
		#
		# SCOPED TO A LOGIN BASKFY DID NOT START. `POST /brokers/{id}/connect` mints a `state`
		# and the callback route requires one back, so a Baskfy-initiated flow carries `state`
		# and falls through to the API untouched. This matcher catches only the other case: a
		# bare `request_token` arriving from Zerodha with no state — which is what the
		# operator's morning Kite login produces.
		#
		# The server-side twin of this hop, for a login with no browser to redirect, is
		# `/opt/baskfy/bin/baskfy-desk-handoff` -> the desk's `accept-kite-request-token` forced
		# command. See `tools/deploy/desk/README.md`.
		@kite_login_return {
			path /api/v1/brokers/callback
			query request_token=*
			not query state=*
		}
		redir @kite_login_return {$BASKFY_DESK_CALLBACK_URL:https://desk.modelbasket.in/callback}?request_token={http.request.uri.query.request_token} 302

		handle /api/v1/* {
			reverse_proxy api:8000 {
				# docs/08 §5: "the backtest SSE stream goes to api.baskfy.com directly on the ALB (idle
				# timeout raised to 300 s), not through CloudFront". No CloudFront in Phase A, so the
				# equivalent is: never buffer, and do not time the stream out mid-backtest.
				flush_interval -1
				transport http {
					response_header_timeout 300s
				}
			}
		}

		# Everything else is the Next.js app.
		handle {
			reverse_proxy web:3000 {
				flush_interval -1
			}
		}
	}

	# An error response never reaches the `header` directive above, no matter where it is placed or
	# whether it is deferred (both were tried). `handle_errors` is the supported hook for that path.
	#
	# This was written for `basic_auth`'s 401, which was the one response a stranger ever received.
	# The gate is gone (M46.4) and the block stays, because a 404 or a 502 is now what a stranger
	# meets on a bad URL — and with the password removed, `X-Robots-Tag` is one of only two things
	# still keeping unreviewed legal drafts out of a search index. An error page that quietly lost
	# the header would be a hole in the wrong wall.
	handle_errors {
		header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive, nosnippet"
		header -Server
		respond "{err.status_code} {err.status_text}" {err.status_code}
	}
}
